<template>
    <children>
        <li id="38.lp5"/>
        <li id="30.lp5"/>
    </children>
    <heading>Utilities</heading>
    <keywords>
        <li>script</li>
        <li>function</li>
        <li>template</li>
        <li>mount</li>
        <li>syntax error</li>
        <li>utility</li>
    </keywords>
    <section data-lp5-kind="explanation">
        <p>Template scripts and compiled state machines need to become callable functions with named inputs. <code>scriptToFunction(code, name, ...args)</code> defines such a function without executing its body. It uses a temporary classic script rather than <code>new Function</code> to obtain browser syntax-error line numbers. The wrapper starts the supplied code on the same line so those numbers identify the original source text.</p>
        <p>The generated script needs a way to return its function to the helper. While it executes, <code>document.currentScript</code> identifies its own script element; the temporary <code>fn</code> property is that handoff. This relies on synchronous classic-script execution. Syntax failures are reported with the supplied name, line, and faulty source text.</p>
        <p><code>mountTemplate(tplOrId, parent, ctx)</code> gives each mount its own elements and initialization scope. Its script receives <code>elementByID</code>, a map of the cloned elements, and <code>ctx</code>, the caller's context. Nested templates remain separate mountable units, so their contents are not searched. The first script is extracted and compiled before insertion: syntax failure leaves the parent untouched. Initialization runs after insertion so focus and measurement can work; a runtime failure at that stage does not roll back the attached DOM. The script's result is returned, or <code>null</code> if there is no nonempty script.</p>
    </section>
    <section data-lp5-kind="code">
        <name>JavaScript units</name>
        <code><![CDATA[let util = (() => {
    function scriptToFunction(code, name, ...args) {
        let failure = null;
        const onError = e => { failure = e; };
        addEventListener("error", onError);

        const script = document.createElement("script");
        script.textContent =
            `document.currentScript.fn = function (${args.join(", ")}) {${code}\n};\n` +
            `//# sourceURL=lp5-${name}.js`;

        document.head.append(script); // Runs synchronously.
        script.remove();
        removeEventListener("error", onError);

        // currentScript identifies the executing script element. Its temporary fn property
        // carries the newly defined function back here; defining it did not call its body.
        if (script.fn) return script.fn;

        const line = failure?.lineno || null;
        const sourceLine = line ? (code.split("\n")[line - 1] ?? "").trim() : "";
        throw new SyntaxError(
            `template "${name}" script, line ${line ?? "?"}: ` +
            `${failure?.message ?? "syntax error"}` +
            (sourceLine ? `\n    ${sourceLine}` : "")
        );
    }

    function mountTemplate(tplOrId, parent, ctx) {
        const name = typeof tplOrId === "string" ? tplOrId : tplOrId?.id || "template";
        const tpl = typeof tplOrId === "string"
            ? document.getElementById(tplOrId)
            : tplOrId;
        if (!tpl) throw new Error(`LP5.mountTemplate: no template "${name}"`);

        const clone = document.importNode(tpl.content, true);
        const script = clone.querySelector("script");
        const code = script?.textContent;
        script?.remove();

        const elementByID = {};
        for (const el of clone.querySelectorAll("[id]")) elementByID[el.id] = el;
        const fn = code?.trim()
            ? scriptToFunction(code, name, "elementByID", "ctx")
            : null;

        parent.append(clone);
        return fn ? fn(elementByID, ctx) : null;
    }

    ]]><lp5- ref="Directory handle persistence"/><lp5- ref="Browser database"/><![CDATA[

    return { scriptToFunction, mountTemplate, indexedDB, browserDB };
})();
]]></code>
    </section>
</template>
